 (both backslash\n546\tforms). See the TODO comment by `_CITE_OPENER_TOKENS` in `eval/link_annotator.py`.\n547\tStill open:\n548\t- **Traditional multi-head MTP shortcut:** this model's MTP is a training-only\n549\t  shared-`lm_head` aux loss (skipped at eval), so the high-precision c+ite signal\n550\t  needs an extra forward. A model trained with *separate persistent MTP heads*\n551\t  per offset could read P(c),P(ite) from ONE forward — exploit if such a\n552\t  checkpoint is ever trained.\n553\t- **Markdown merged-punctuation openers** (` ([` 29565, ` \"[` 12878, ...) are\n554\t  deliberately excluded (<2% coverage; injecting them splices malformed markdown).\n555\t  Would need generalized splice logic to include as scan-but-not-inject targets.\n556\t\n557\t### Cross-doc benchmark ports — follow-ups (filed 2026-07-25)\n558\tHarness in eval/benchmark_harness/ + 3 external ports built (see\n559\tdocs/crossdoc_benchmark_port_harness_DESIGN.md verdict summary). Open items:\n560\t- **Kotlin/ASE-2025 Tier-2 — DIAGNOSED 2026-07-25, needs power**: the scope\n561\t  ablation (scopes.py, `--scope all`) settled cause (b): the native miss was\n562\t  arbitrary FIM spans burying the signal. Use-site anchoring recovers a\n563\t  significant placebo separation that strengthens toward the use line\n564\t  (rest_of_doc +0.051 → use_block +0.058 → use_line +0.106, all CI-exclude-0),\n565\t  while native placebo sep is −0.033. See docs/crossdoc_benchmark_port_harness_\n566\t  DESIGN.md \"Kotlin scope-gradient result\". REMAINING to make it a headline\n567\t  benchmark: (1) raise n above the 200 floor — build the port over the FULL\n568\t  ASE public+private split instead of the 500-cap subset (only 138/242 survive\n569\t  the use-site filter); (2) re-run on a stronger kotlin cross_doc_link ckpt to\n570\t  lift Δnll_real (cross-vs-flat) CIs above 0 — currently still include 0 even\n571\t  though placebo separation is solid. use_line or use_block is the scope to\n572\t  report.\n573\t- **TS/CrossCodeEval v2 whole-file aux**: current v1 uses retrieval chunks\n574\t  (fire 0.40, Δnll_real CI barely incl 0, but placebo sep CI EXCLUDES 0 —\n575\t  promising). Re-clone repos from metadata.repository for whole-file aux +\n576\t  runtime relative-import resolution; target fire ~0.67 and a clean Δnll_real\n577\t  pass. Also consider a Tier-1 runtime-resolution mode so the static gate stops\n578\t  under-firing on relative-import langs (advisory-only today).\n579\t- **Go/CoLT-132K unblock**: email aixcoder authors for the external `godata`\n580\t  dependency JSONs (dependency_file_path), OR build Go via the self-built repo-\n581\t  snapshot path (like Kotlin/ASE). Adapter ports/colt_go.py is ready for the\n582\t  former. NOT registered in ports/__init__.py until unblocked.\n583\t- Then wire the passing ports into eval_checkpoints.py as first-class benchmarks\n584\t  (like repobench_cross_doc) so sweeps report them automatically.\n585\t\n586\t### Resume latent bugs (found 2026-08-07 by resume-corruption audit; NOT the 16B-degradation cause)\n587\tTwo real latent resume bugs surfaced while diagnosing the 16B degradation (which was\n588\tactually LR-too-hot-at-length, not resume). Neither affected the merged_v2 runs\n589\t(explicit max_optimizer_steps + 0 skipped), but both are footguns:\n590\t1. **NULL-path cooldown never engages.** `main.py:1195` `total_steps_original =\n591\t   max_steps_for_cooldown + resumed_steps`. On `max_optimizer_steps: null` (auto-derive\n592\t   from n_packs) resume, the 552-560 `remaining = _max - resumed_steps` adjustment is\n593\t   skipped (it needs an explicit `_max`), so the auto-derive re-computes FULL and +R\n594\t   inflates total to FULL+R → cooldown_start pushed past run end → LR pinned at PEAK for\n595\t   the whole post-resume segment, compounding per resume. Untie can also never fire.\n596\t   Fix: apply `remaining = derived - resumed_steps` after auto-derive too, OR decouple\n597\t   the schedule-total (always FULL) from the loader-cap (FULL - resumed_steps).\n598\t2. **Silent optimizer partial-restore + bf16 mantissa truncation.** `main.py:1100`\n599\t   `load_state_dict_full` leaves name/shape-mismatched params cold and only logs at\n600\t   INFO; a skipped Muon bf16 param also gets its mantissa low-bits zeroed\n601\t   (`optimizers/muon.py:154-156`), truncating fp32-effective → bf16 that resume. Add a\n602\t   hard `assert skipped == []` on resume (currently only INFO-logged).\n603\t","tool_use_id":"toolu_01MxMv1JXsQ7EoXr52FFf4Ra","type":"tool_result"}],"role":"user"},"context":{"branch":"main","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/tagseq2tagseq","head":"5c15f9d0ec9630286ce8d7d66c05e849ae72097f","repo":"git@github.com:evintunador/tagseq2tagseq.git"},"conversation":{"id":"claude-code:5cbbe416-90e8-4a21-93e7-044acf862880","seq":30},"id":"ev1-74dd9629694abdedc29b93e5a5d8c8e3d2112f349e44cc09080b10e17bd70f7f","kind":"conversation_turn","occurred_at":"2026-08-24T03:00:36.934Z","producer":{"session_id":"5cbbe416-90e8-4a21-93e7-044acf862880","source":"claude-code","source_version":"2.1.241","tool":"cledger","version":"0.25.0"},"raw":{"data":{"cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/tagseq2tagseq/paper","entrypoint":"cli","gitBranch":"main","isSidechain":false,"message":{"content":[{"content":"1\t# TODO\n2\t\n3\tRemaining work, organized by area. All completed items stripped.\n4\t\n5\t---\n6\t\n7\t## Java dataset — cross-repo framework-class mock resolution\n8\t\n9\tIn the Java code dataset, framework/stdlib imports (`android.content.Context`,\n10\t`java.util.List`, etc.) can RESOLVE at generation/eval time to *another repo's*\n11\tmock/stub reimplementation of that class, because Java FQNs are a global namespace\n12\tand several repos in the corpus define their own `android.*` / `java.*` stubs.\n13\tOnly ~0.8% of nodes are framework-namespaced, and the TRAINING graph is unaffected\n14\t(its edges are intra-repo by construction — see `build_java_graph.build_repo_nodes`);\n15\tthis only touches the `PretokCorpus` generation/eval resolver, which matches an\n16\temitted import against ALL nodes. Look into whether to (a) drop framework-prefixed\n17\tnodes from the corpus, (b) scope generation-time resolution to the active repo, or\n18\t(c) leave it. Surfaced 2026-07-19 via `run_sample_dump` on the Go/Java datasets;\n19\tsee `docs/multilang_code_datasets_DESIGN.md` §13 (Java quality nuance).\n20\t\n21\t**Verified 2026-07-20 (two adversarial reviewers of `01_sample_dump.txt`):** in the\n22\tsample-dump, ~71% of resolved links are framework/stdlib FQNs matching a foreign\n23\trepo's mock/stub (0% resolved to the WRONG class — it's exact-FQN, so no\n24\tmis-resolution, just semantically-empty \"stub magnet\" edges); only ~29% are genuine\n25\tintra-project deps. BUT this is a `PretokCorpus`-resolver artifact — spot-checked\n26\tthe STORED training graph and `java.util.List`/`Map`/`android.content.Context` each\n27\thave their few in-edges all from the SINGLE repo that vendored the stub (intra-repo\n28\tby construction), so TRAINING edges are NOT contaminated. Fix is only needed for the\n29\tgeneration/eval path. Also: wildcard imports (`import a.b.*;`) are intentionally\n30\tdropped by the detector (a package has no single file node) — confirm that's the\n31\tdesired behavior or add package-info handling.\n32\t\n33\t---\n34\t\n35\t## Data\n36\t\n37\t### Wikipedia redirect map\n38\tThe Wikipedia dump ships a `redirect.sql` table mapping stub redirect titles to\n39\ttheir canonical targets (e.g. \"UK\" → \"United Kingdom\"). Fix at graph construction\n40\ttime: rewrite in-text `[anchor](RedirectTitle)` links to the canonical node's title\n41\tand drop redirect stub nodes entirely. Downstream benefit: `HashNormTitleIndex`\n42\thits these titles directly, fixing the class of eval misses where the model generates\n43\ta redirect title that isn't a first-class node.\n44\t\n45\t### Merge all datasets → diversity-scaling experiment (REDESIGNED 2026-07-29)\n46\t**Supersedes the old wiki+stack+arxiv+fineweb merge.** First-principles rethink:\n47\tthe claim is \"cross-doc attention helps graph-structured text, and the benefit\n48\tsurvives/strengthens when many link types are learned jointly (diversity) and as\n49\tdata scales.\" Fineweb is DROPPED everywhere — it is edgeless, so (a) a fineweb-only\n50\trun has no cross-doc A/B (it's just a different corpus, uninterpretable vs the\n51\tmerge) and (b) inside the merge its tokens are seen identically by both masks, so\n52\tthey only DILUTE the measurable Δ. \"General intelligence\" was never the claim.\n53\t\n54\t**Design:** 11 linked sources (wiki, arxiv, python/thestack, typescript, javascript,\n55\tkotlin, rust, go, java, dart, zig), NO fineweb. Equal-ish token split (the balance\n56\tIS the variable under test; every source now has an internal cross-doc benchmark\n57\tthat match/exceed the external ports, so no signal-weighting needed). Each rung is\n58\ta cross_doc_link vs doc_causal PAIR (within-model Δ = the interpretable metric,\n59\trobust to LR/batch since both arms share them). Fixed recipe carried from the small\n60\truns (muon_lr=0.003, muon_wd=0.1, adamw_lr=3e-5, 262144 tok/step) — only the step\n61\tbudget changes across rungs.\n62\t\n63\t**Rungs (token-scaling line):**\n64\t- 3.9B — exact COMPUTE-match to the small single-source runs (15k steps × 262k tok);\n65\t  the clean diversity control. Equal split ≈ 11809 packs/source (zig gives all 921).\n66\t  No extra precompute (every source has ≥1 epoch at this size).\n67\t- 8B — scaling point. ~27256 packs for the 7 big sources; go/java/dart/zig give all.\n68\t  No extra precompute.\n69\t- 16B ×2 — (a) perfectly-balanced multi-epoch and (b) take-what-it's-got (big\n70\t  sources absorb the remainder, small give 1 epoch). Bonus ablation: isolates\n71\t  whether BALANCE per se matters at fixed total tokens.\n72\t\n73\t**Multi-epoch = DISTINCT-SEED epochs, NOT epoch_0 replay (confirmed 2026-07-30).**\n74\tprecompute_epochs.py writes epoch_i with seed=base+i; the seed drives worker\n75\tpartition + BFS re-seed + traversal order, so each epoch is a genuinely different\n76\tpacking over the full graph (verified: zig epoch_0/1/2 = seeds 42/43/44, different\n77\tpack counts + doc_id fingerprints). So repeating epochs adds new co-occurrence\n78\tstructure — real signal, not duplication. Multi-epoch schedules ALREADY EXIST on\n79\tdisk from the traversal-ablation work: zig 16ep, dart 9, java 7, go 6, wiki/rust 4,\n80\tkotlin 2; thestack/arxiv/typescript/javascript have only epoch_0 (fine — they're\n81\tcapped, never repeated).\n82\t\n83\t**16B perfectly-balanced allocation (SAFE=4 epoch-repeat cap; NO top-up precompute\n84\tneeded — all reachable from on-disk epochs):** equal-ish share, but zig can't reach\n85\t1/11 (would need ~48 epochs of a 59M-tok corpus) so it's capped at 4 epochs = 3,618\n86\tpacks = 0.12B; its shortfall redistributes to the other 10 → 48,466 packs = 1.59B\n87\teach. Total 488,278 packs = 16.00B. Epochs used per source: arxiv/stack/ts/js 0.1-0.3\n88\t(capped, epoch_0 only), kotlin 0.6, rust 1.3, wiki 1.7, go 2.2, java 2.9, dart 3.5\n89\t(tightest, <4), zig 4 (capped). SAFE=4 per Muennighoff-style 2-4-epochs-near-lossless.\n90\t\n91\t**Rungs are INDEPENDENT, NOT nested (decided 2026-07-30).** Each rung\n92\tbalance-samples its own target (like the 3.9B build). Dropped strict nesting\n93\t(3.9B⊄8B⊄16B) — it would need a prefix-stable selector (deterministic per-bucket\n94\torder + proportional prefixes) and a 3.9B rebuild. ACCEPTED CONFOUND: a Δ-vs-budget\n95\tchange across rungs could partly reflect pack RESAMPLING, not pure scale. Note this\n96\tin the paper. The 3.9B rung (epoch_3p9b, already built + training as jobs 50016/50017)\n97\tis NOT rebuilt.\n98\t\n99\t**merge_packs.py still needs a small change for 16B:** accept MULTIPLE epoch dirs per\n100\t--source (comma-sep), union them (each epoch's packs are distinct; keep all, reassign\n101\tpack_id across the union), then _select_balanced the target across the union. Current\n102\tcode takes one dir/source. Not needed for 3.9B/8B (single epoch each), only the\n103\tperfectly-balanced 16B. take-what-it's-got 16B also single-epoch (epoch_0 + caps).\n104\t\n105\tBuild: merge_datasets over 11 sources' splits/train → merged_all_v2/splits/*;\n106\tmerge_packs.py (already source-generic: tag=train_dir=schedule_dir=target, auto-reads\n107\tper-source layout+detector from schedule metadata) with per-source targets → one\n108\tepoch dir per rung. Per-source val schedules for the 8 new langs via\n109\tscripts/precompute_merged_v2_val.sh. Eval per-source on 11 internal benchmarks +\n110\t5 external ports. See memory [[merged-corpus-build]].\n111\t\n112\t### Epochs-to-degradation: does cross-doc tolerate MORE epochs than doc-causal? (filed 2026-07-30)\n113\tHypothesis: doc_causal repetition replays near-identical 32k windows (same doc, same\n114\taccidental neighbors) → memorizes fast (Muennighoff ~2-4 epochs then degrade). But\n115\tcross_doc_link re-samples the DAG TRAVERSAL each epoch (distinct precompute seed →\n116\teach epoch packs a doc with a DIFFERENT subset/order of its linked neighbors), so the\n117\tCONDITIONING CONTEXT for the same tokens changes every epoch — combinatorially many\n118\t\"views\" scaling with graph connectivity, not 1. Prediction: cdl's val-loss upturn\n119\t(overfit onset) happens at a HIGHER epoch count than dc's on the identical corpus =\n120\ta shifted/shallower degradation curve (NOT infinite epochs — finite tokens still\n121\tmemorize eventually). Experiment: one dense-graph single-source corpus (kotlin or go\n122\t— dense import graph, go already has 6 epochs on disk), {cdl, dc} × {1,2,4,8,16}\n123\tepochs, SAME schedule seeds so ONLY the mask differs (fair — the seed reshuffles dc\n124\tpacks too). Plot held-out val loss + a memorization probe (train-val gap or train\n125\tverbatim recall) vs epochs per mask; the divergence point IS the result. Distinct\n126\tfrom the diversity ladder + edge-dropout line. Uses on-disk multi-epoch schedules.\n127\t\n128\t### Edge-dropout density line (cross-doc Δ vs graph connectivity) — filed 2026-07-29\n129\tThe principled version of \"how much better could this get with a denser corpus?\"\n130\tHold the corpus COMPLETELY fixed (same docs/tokens/order) and vary ONLY the fraction\n131\tof resolved links the mask may use: keep 100/75/50/25/0% of grants, seeded. At 0% it\n132\tdegenerates to doc_causal (sanity: the line should hit the doc_causal baseline). This\n133\tis the clean instrument — NOT interpolating %fineweb, which confounds density with\n134\tcontent/quality/distribution (fineweb is different, higher-quality text; adding it\n135\tchanges content, not just connectivity, and %-fineweb isn't even monotone in density).\n136\tThe resulting line \"cross-doc Δ vs connectivity\" extrapolates the payoff of a denser,\n137\tbetter-connected corpus than the ones we have. Impl: subsample each pack's\n138\t`link_to_target` grants with a seeded RNG.\n139\t- EVAL-TIME dropping (cheap: 1 trained cross_doc_link ckpt, N evals) answers \"how much\n140\t  does the model USE density.\"\n141\t- TRAIN-TIME dropping (N runs) answers \"how much does density HELP LEARNING\" — the\n142\t  stronger paper claim, N× cost.\n143\tCheapest at mask-build time (subsample grants before the backend split), not graph\n144\tbuild. Do after the merged run lands.\n145\t\n146\t### Short LR check at the 16B rung (before cross-rung Δ claims) — filed 2026-07-29\n147\tWithin a rung the cross_doc-vs-doc_causal Δ is LR-robust (both arms share LR). But\n148\tcomparing Δ ACROSS rungs (3.9B vs 16B) could be biased if a longer schedule wants a\n149\tdifferent peak LR. Model size is FIXED across rungs (no μP/width-transfer issue), so\n150\tsensitivity is mild — plan is fixed muon_lr=0.003 + extend schedule. Insurance: a\n151\tquick 2–3 value muon_lr check (~{0.002,0.003,0.004}) at 16B, pick by subset val_loss,\n152\tbefore trusting cross-rung Δ comparisons.\n153\t\n154\t### Batch-size sweep (absolute efficiency, NOT the diversity experiment) — filed 2026-07-29\n155\t262144 tok/step (8×32k×accum1) was inherited from ../moddednanogpt (tuned for a 124M\n156\tGPT-2 on FineWeb), never tuned for the ~350M model here. It does NOT bias the\n157\twithin-model cross-doc Δ (both arms share it) and re-tuning would break the 3.9B\n158\tcompute-match to the small runs, so it stays fixed for the scaling experiment. File a\n159\tSEPARATE batch-size sweep only if absolute token-efficiency becomes a goal.\n160\t\n161\t### Preprocess code data to make imports lazy\n162\tRewrite The Stack code so imports are lazy, saving compute (fewer/later-resolved\n163\timport edges to traverse and attend to). Investigate whether this meaningfully\n164\tshrinks the link closure for cross_doc_link packing.\n165\t\n166\t### Make thestack datasets of other programming languages\n167\texpand TheStack beyond Python to include all available \n168\tcoding languages. Grab more languages and build link detectors + import-graph\n169\textractors for each (JS/TS `import`/`require`, Ruby `require`, Go imports, etc.),\n170\textending the Python-only import graph in `model/graph_traversal/link_detector.py`\n171\t— or use a language-agnostic call-graph approach.\n172\t\n173\t---\n174\t\n175\t## Model\n176\t\n177\t### RoPE length extrapolation past the 32k training window (later — consider)\n178\tThe rotary cos/sin buffer is sized to max_seq_len=32768 and\n179\t`flex_self_attention` hard-asserts `cos.size(0) >= T`, so any pack over 32k\n180\taborts (hit during cross-doc benchmark scoring on whole-file Kotlin aux; the\n181\tTier-2 harness now skips+counts oversized packs as a workaround, and\n182\t`rest_of_doc`-scope benchmark packs are the ones that bump this). Consider\n183\tediting the RoPE code/config to let the model EXTRAPOLATE beyond its training\n184\tlength — e.g. NTK-aware / YaRN / linear position-interpolation scaling of the\n185\trotary frequencies, or just building a longer cos/sin table at inference — so\n186\tlong-context eval (and generation) isn't clipped at the training window. Would\n187\tlet benchmarks with large whole-file aux score without truncation and is a\n188\tprerequisite for any >32k context experiments.\n189\t\n190\t### Link-utilization vs. packing-distance diagnostic (RoPE-offset concern — filed 2026-08-10)\n191\tWe use global RoPE positions [0,T) with no per-doc reset, so a granted target sits at\n192\ta packing-order-dependent relative offset. zhao2024analysing resets RoPE per document,\n193\tbut only because its IntraDoc *bans* cross-doc attention — a clean reset is ill-defined\n194\tonce grants cross boundaries (one key, one absolute position, read by linkers at many\n195\tdistances). So assess the concern empirically instead of ablating a reset. On existing\n196\tcheckpoints (no retrain): measure attention mass on the granted target (link\n197\tutilization) as a function of link->target packing distance, per source. If utilization\n198\tis flat across distance, the concern is empirically dead and it becomes one appendix\n199\tsentence. If it decays, that motivates the per-grant embedding arm below. Appendix\n200\tresult. See `paper/notes/synthesis_framing_notes.md` (RoPE section) for the framing.\n201\t\n202\t### Per-grant additive position embedding (only if the diagnostic shows decay — filed 2026-08-10)\n203\tLarger follow-up to the utilization diagnostic above, gated on it showing distance\n204\tdecay. Vanilla RoPE can't re-base a granted key to a canonical offset per querying edge.\n205\tA second, additive embedding tagging \"this is a granted cross-doc token\" (NoPE-style /\n206\tlearned per-grant tag) sidesteps RoPE entirely. This is a real research arm (new\n207\tembedding, retrain), not a quick ablation — only pursue if the cheap diagnostic\n208\tjustifies it. Appendix result.\n209\t\n210\t---\n211\t\n212\t## Generation / Inference\n213\t\n214\t### Prompt-link resolution can fabricate cited docs (design decision — consider)\n215\tWith defaults (`process_prompt_links=True` and a `link_retrieval_mode` that allows\n216\tgeneration), a link merely *cited* in the prompt that isn't in the corpus (or with\n217\t`corpus=None`) falls through `_handle_link` to the recursive-generation branch and\n218\t*hallucinates* a whole document for that identifier, inserting it before the root\n219\tso root generation attends to fabricated content. If the intent is \"resolve\n220\texisting citations, don't fabricate them\", prompt-link processing should force\n221\t`corpus_only` semantics. Confirm intended behavior before changing.\n222\t\n223\t### Per-token detector routing within one mixed-syntax document (\"Tier 2\") — OUT OF SCOPE (future direction)\n224\tDeferred and explicitly **out of scope for this project** — recorded as an interesting\n225\tfuture direction, not a work item here. `CompositeLinkDetector` (built 2026-08-01,\n226\t`model/graph_traversal/composite_link_detector.py`) dispatches ONE sub-detector *per\n227\tdocument*, chosen by identifier/content sniff. A single document that genuinely **mixes\n228\tlink syntaxes** — e.g. a markdown article embedding a `\\cite{}`, a literate-programming\n229\tfile interleaving prose links and code imports, or a README with both `[text](url)` and\n230\tfenced code `import`s — is classified by its dominant language and detected with that one\n231\tsub-detector, so the minority syntax's links are missed. A proper fix is **per-token (or\n232\tper-span) detector routing within one sequence**: segment the document by language region\n233\t(fenced code blocks, LaTeX environments, etc.) and run the matching detector on each\n234\tregion, merging the results. This is the deferred \"Tier 2\" from the merged-corpus design\n235\t([[merged-corpus-build]]). No current use case needs it — qualitative single-root\n236\tgeneration and the single-language benchmark ports are all dominant-language — and it\n237\twould add real complexity (region segmentation, offset bookkeeping across regions,\n238\tcross-firing between adjacent regions). Revisit only if a downstream task requires\n239\tfaithful multi-syntax detection inside one document.\n240\t\n241\t### TheStack (Python) link resolution in generation is unsupported\n242\t`generate.py` / `model/generation_loop.py` resolve a detected link to a corpus doc\n243\tvia `corpus.has_document(target)` (exact → detector-key → optional fuzzy cascade).\n244\tThis works for Wikipedia (`[text](Title)`) and ArXiv (`\\cite{Title}`) because the\n245\tdetector's `target_str` equals the corpus `raw_identifier`. Fuzzy matching does not\n246\thelp here — the mismatch is a structural key-format difference, not a near-miss.\n247\tIt does **not** work for TheStack: the\n248\t`PythonImportDetector` emits *relative* import paths (e.g. `\"Phaedra/Notebook.py\"`)\n249\twhile corpus `raw_identifier`s are repo-qualified (`\"000alen/Phaedra:Phaedra/Notebook.py\"`),\n250\tso corpus hits never fire on a multi-repo dataset. See the NOTE in\n251\t`generate.py::PretokCorpus.has_document`. Fix options: (a) build a single-repo\n252\tcorpus so identifiers match, or (b) make the import detector emit repo-qualified\n253\tidentifiers when a repo context is available. Until then, Python-link generation\n254\tfalls back to generate/skip per `link_retrieval_mode` (no corpus fetch).\n255\t\n256\t### Merged-model (composite) link RESOLUTION across all code sources (filed 2026-08-01)\n257\t`CompositeLinkDetector` (built 2026-08-01) closes link *detection* for merged-model\n258\tgeneration — it routes per-document to the right sub-detector, so links in wiki /\n259\tarxiv / all 9 code languages are all detected (verified: cross-distribution smoke,\n260\t10/11 sources fire in their own syntax). But *resolution* — turning a detected\n261\t`target_str` into an actual corpus fetch via `corpus.has_document(target)` — is a\n262\tSEPARATE, still-open step, and it generalizes the Python-specific note above to every\n263\tcode source. The same structural key-format mismatch applies: the code detectors emit\n264\trelative / repo-*un*qualified targets (python `chess/board.py`, ts `src/util/helper`,\n265\trust `crate::net::tcp`, dart `lib/models/user.dart`, …) while a multi-repo corpus's\n266\t`raw_identifier`s are repo-qualified (`owner/repo:...` / `owner/repo@...`). Wiki and\n267\tarxiv resolve fine (target == identifier); code sources never hit on a multi-repo\n268\tcorpus. So merged-model generation today can DETECT a code link but not FETCH its\n269\ttarget — it falls back to generate/skip per `link_retrieval_mode`.\n270\tFix options (same shape as the Python note, applied corpus-wide): (a) generate against\n271\ta SINGLE-repo corpus per language (`data/make_repo_corpus.py`) so identifiers match the\n272\tdetectors' relative keys; or (b) thread a repo context into `detect_links_for_doc` /\n273\tthe composite so it can emit repo-qualified targets; or (c) add a source-aware\n274\tresolution index in `PretokCorpus` that matches on the detector-key form\n275\t(`index_doc_span`) per source rather than the raw identifier. Option (c) is the most\n276\tgeneral and mirrors how the training/eval match already works (`_match_links_to_docs`\n277\tuses `index_doc_span`). Note the composite already implements per-source\n278\t`index_doc_span`, so a resolution index keyed on it is the natural hook. Until done,\n279\tuse `--link-retrieval-mode link_but_skip` (detect-only) for merged-model smokes, or a\n280\tsingle-repo corpus for real code retrieval. Detection is DONE; this is resolution only.\n281\t\n282\t---\n283\t\n284\t## Training\n285\t\n286\t### Retune LR / schedule for this dataset scale (before next ablation run)\n287\tCurrent optimizer/schedule values (muon_lr, adamw_lr, warmup, cooldown_frac,\n288\ttotal_steps) are inherited from ../ModdedNanoGPT, which tunes for how much a\n289\tmodel learns in ~the first hour of training — mis-scaled for our dataset sizes\n290\tand multi-day runs. The 2026-07-01..07 arxiv/thestack/wiki ablation runs were all\n291\tundertrained/poorly-tuned as a result (see RESULTS.md — barely-above-chance).\n292\tRetune before spending GPU-time on the next matrix.\n293\t\n294\t### Train the ablation matrix\n295\tActually train reasonable-sized models for each ablation:\n296\t(random, random-walk, dfs, bfs) × (doc-causal, cross-doc-link). NOTE: `random`\n297\tstrategy was previously introduced without approval and its runs deleted — confirm\n298\tthe intended strategy set before committing GPU-time (BFS is the established one).\n299\t\n300\t### Automate the compile-cache warmup (TODO)\n301\tMulti-rank/multi-node runs require a pre-warmed shared compile cache to avoid the\n302\tconcurrent-compilation segfault (see `launch_slurm.py`: `TS2TS_SHARED_COMPILE_CACHE`\n303\t+ `TORCHINDUCTOR_COMPILE_THREADS=1`). Today this is a manual two-step: warm the\n304\tcache once with a short run at the target world_size, then point the real run at\n305\tthe same `TS2TS_SHARED_COMPILE_CACHE`. Fold this into `launch_slurm.py` as an\n306\tautomatic `--warmup-compile` pre-step (submit a brief warmup job at the target\n307\tworld_size, wait, then launch the real job against the warmed cache). The warmup\n308\tmust match world_size: the distributed Muon optimizer compiles shard-shape kernels\n309\ta single-GPU warmup never produces.\n310\t\n311\t### Live PackedSequenceDataset: dedup docs within an epoch (TODO — consider)\n312\tThe live `PackedSequenceDataset` / `PackBatchSampler` path samples WITH\n313\treplacement: seeds are drawn via `self._rng.randrange(num_nodes)` and dedup is\n314\tonly *within* a pack (`pack_doc_ids`), with no cross-pack/epoch visited set. So a\n315\tdoc can appear in many packs and some may never appear in a given pass. The\n316\tprecomputed path already dedups per epoch (`epoch_precompute.py` `epoch_visited`\n317\tset → visited docs read as tok_len=0). Consider giving the live path the same\n318\t\"each doc at most once per epoch\" guarantee (a persistent visited set on the\n319\tsampler, reset per epoch) so the two paths match and data usage is even. Note the\n320\tinteraction with truncation: a doc dropped/partially-used by pack-level trimming\n321\tshould arguably remain eligible until its body is actually consumed.\n322\t\n323\t### Parallelized eval in main.py\n324\t`run_benchmarks_on_model` runs serially. Naïve thread-pool parallelism is risky:\n325\tbenchmarks vary widely in runtime (HellaSwag ~30s vs. community_pack_perplexity\n326\t~20min), so fast workers block waiting for slow ones — net win near zero, timeout\n327\trisk real. Better design: shared job queue (`queue.Queue`) where each worker pulls\n328\tthe next unstarted benchmark, so fast workers don't sit idle. All workers share the\n329\tsame compiled model (no re-compile). Implement only if eval wall-time becomes a\n330\tbottleneck.\n331\t\n332\t---\n333\t\n334\t## Eval\n335\t\n336\t### Leakage-stratified cross-doc Δnll (RETRO bpb(α) protocol — filed 2026-08-10)\n337\tOur grant makes verbatim copying from the target more direct than retrieval baselines,\n338\tand dedup is sampling-only, so raw perplexity gains risk being re-exposure of\n339\tmemorized/duplicated text (HotpotQA's 2017 Wikipedia and The Stack both overlap\n340\ttraining data). Measure FIRST on the already-completed sweep (pure eval, no retrain):\n341\tstratify the cross-doc Δnll by target<->context n-gram overlap α and show the effect\n342\tsurvives at low overlap. Only if the low-overlap effect is weak do we go back to the\n343\tdatasets, filter, and re-run training — a HARD BLOCKER on final numbers *conditional*\n344\ton the measurement demanding it, NOT a pre-committed retrain. Expect a steeper leakage\n345\tslope than RETRO. (Related but distinct from the deferred dataset-side dedup blacklist\n346\titem below, which is graph-construction dedup, not eval-side α-stratification.)\n347\t\n348\t### Placebo/derangement on the headline arms + fired-subset Δnll (filed 2026-08-10)\n349\tTier-2 ports already have the derangement placebo (`eval/benchmark_harness/tier2.py`,\n350\t`placebo_separation` + bootstrap CI). The headline HotpotQA-cross-doc and\n351\tRepoBench-cross-doc arms do NOT — they report only cross-vs-flat, where the cross arm\n352\tsees strictly more tokens, so \"is it the right doc or just more context?\" is unanswered\n353\t(acute given HotpotQA single-hop solvability). Extend the existing derangement machinery\n354\tto the headline arms, or caveat prominently. Also report Δnll over the *fired* subset\n355\thonestly (Repoformer's ~20/60/20 help/neutral/hurt split makes averaging over non-fired\n356\titems misleading).\n357\t\n358\t### Eval-time max_seq_len extension (long-context generalization probe — filed 2026-08-10)\n359\tCheap probe, no retrain: eval a 32k-trained checkpoint at larger max_seq_len (64k+) and\n360\tcheck whether grants keep helping as context grows past the training window. Direct\n361\tlong-context-generalization signal for cross-doc attention. NOTE: blocked by the same\n362\t`cos.size(0) >= T` RoPE-buffer assert as the \"RoPE length extrapolation\" Model item —\n363\teither build a longer cos/sin table at inference or land the extrapolation work first.\n364\t\n365\t### Finish the concat controls across non-bfs traversal (filed 2026-08-10)\n366\tThe traversal ablation grid is `{bfs, dfs, random_walk, random} x {dc, cdl}`, but the\n367\tconcat controls (`doc_concatenated`, `doc_concat_link`) exist ONLY at bfs (every concat\n368\tconfig pins `strategy: 'bfs'`). `doc_concatenated` has no links, so traversal strategy\n369\tstill legitimately varies *which docs co-pack* — running concat across dfs/random_walk/\n370\trandom is a real control (\"does co-packing selection matter independent of the mask?\").\n371\tEither finish those cells or document why bfs-only concat is sufficient. Folds into the\n372\t\"Train the ablation matrix\" item under Training.\n373\t\n374\t### `annotated` (link-injection) eval speed — PARTIALLY DONE, remaining levers\n375\tThe `annotated` condition (inject `[text](Title)` / `\\cite{Title}` links into\n376\tbenchmark prompts, then let cross-doc attention pull the linked corpus doc) was\n377\t~1s+/item. Two fixes landed (2026-07, commits da0f530..bbd1eac):\n378\t- **C (done):** dropped `edit_distance` from `annotator_strategies` in all configs.\n379\t  It did an O(9.6M) rapidfuzz scan per prompt (~338ms → ~5ms/lookup, 71×). Only\n380\t  typo-recall lost (91%→4% on synthetic typos; a trained model rarely emits those).\n381\t  Still available in code + `link_fuzzy_strategies` (generation-time, per-link).\n382\t- **B (done):** removed a redundant forward pass in `MarkdownPromptAnnotator.annotate`.\n383\t\n384\t**REMAINING (the dominant cost is now autoregressive title generation, ~60\n385\tsequential forwards/item on arxiv, no KV cache — NOT the lookup anymore):**\n386\t- **A — batch the Step-1 opener scan forwards** across benchmark items. Modest\n387\t  win (~5–10%); the scan is a small slice of total forwards.\n388\t- **D — bound/​batch title generation** (the real remaining cost): lower\n389\t  `max_title_tokens` (50/60 is generous; real titles are short) and/or batch the\n390\t  autoregressive title gen. `forward_inference` is hardwired to B=1 (assert in\n391\t  attention.py); the batching pattern to mirror is `eval/scoring.py`\n392\t  `score_completions_batched` (packs K seqs into one `[1, total_T]` doc_causal\n393\t  forward). Biggest remaining speedup but most invasive.\n394\t\n395\t**NOT YET VERIFIED end-to-end:** the C+B wins were measured component-level\n396\t(lookup latency, forward counts) but a full `annotated` benchmark at realistic\n397\t`n` was never re-run to confirm wall-clock actually dropped as projected. Do this\n398\tbefore assuming the \"annotated eval is slow\" problem is closed.\n399\t\n400\t### Integrate easy LLM benchmarks\n401\tWire in easy LLM benchmarks — likely specific sub-tasks from larger suites (e.g.\n402\tMMLU sub-tasks) that these model sizes can handle, preferably tasks that benefit\n403\tfrom cross-document understanding. (Some already wired: hellaswag, boolq,\n404\topenbookqa in the arxiv config's eval block.)\n405\t\n406\t### Multi-hop QA beyond 2-hop (future / low priority)\n407\tHotpotQA is strictly 2-hop. Deeper graph traversal (BFS depth ≥ 3) is a core\n408\tclaim of the system but is untested. Candidates:\n409\t- **MuSiQue** (`datasets` id: `musique`) — up to 4-hop, ~20k items, English Wikipedia.\n410\t- **2WikiMultiHopQA** (`datasets` id: `locuslab/2WikiMultiHopQA`) — up to 5-hop.\n411\t\n412\tBefore implementing: check dataset availability on cluster and leakage vs. training\n413\tdata (Wikipedia models).\n414\t\n415\t### Synthetic intra-repo cross-doc benchmark for Stack models (designed 2026-07)\n416\tRepoBench cross-doc shows only ~0.4% NLL improvement at early training — good\n417\tsignal but small headline number. **The feasible path for code is a synthetic\n418\tintra-repo benchmark, NOT a link annotator** (link *injection* is the wrong\n419\tabstraction for code: an `import` can't be spliced mid-snippet the way\n420\t`[text](Title)` can, and it must be positional + semantically used). Design:\n421\ttake file B that imports file A, score B's tokens (specifically the spans that\n422\t*use* A's symbols) under two conditions — (1) B alone (doc_causal), (2) A\n423\tprovided as a cross-doc aux (the real import edge). NLL delta measures whether\n424\tthe model exploits the dependency. No annotator, no injection, no labelling —\n425\tthe edge already exists in the repo graph; you present or withhold the real\n426\tneighbor. Reuses `score_completion_with_context_docs` (already used by the\n427\tannotated path) but skips all `annotate()` machinery. Depends on a single-repo\n428\tcorpus so identifiers match (see `data/make_repo_corpus.py`). Alt candidate:\n429\tCodeSearchNet with cross-file call graphs.\n430\t\n431\t### Create + re-run Go/Java (multi-language) cross-doc benchmarks (RAISED 2026-07-21 — action item)\n432\t**Why now:** the 2026-07-21 code cross-doc sweep (see `RESULTS_code_crossdoc.md`) left\n433\tthe Go and Java cross-doc claims **INCONCLUSIVE**. Python confirmed the thesis via\n434\t`repobench_cross_doc` (Δnll +0.135), but that benchmark is Python-hardcoded — it asserts\n435\t`isinstance(model.link_detector, PythonImportDetector)` and loads `tianyang/repobench_python_v1.1`\n436\t(see `eval/nlp_benchmarks.py::run_repobench_cross_doc`, ~L966-982). Go/Java had to fall back\n437\tto `community_pack_perplexity`, which is **near-noise for code** (deltas 0.0002–0.03; Java\n438\tsparsest graph ≈0) because import-graph neighborhoods are too predictable. So we have NO\n439\tdiscriminating cross-doc signal for Go/Java yet.\n440\t\n441\t**To do:**\n442\t- ~~Split `run_repobench_cross_doc` by language and dispatch to the matching\n443\t  `<Lang>ImportDetector`.~~ **DONE 2026-07-23** (commit 91cb33f): `language` param +\n444\t  `_REPOBENCH_LANGUAGES` (python, java) + `--repobench-language`. Java fix: strip the\n445\t  build source root from snippet paths so import FQNs resolve (`_repobench_aux_identifier`).\n446\t  Provisional Java results in `RESULTS_code_crossdoc.md` — cross_doc_link beats flat on\n447\t  every traversal (Δnll +0.065..+0.194), the discriminating signal community_pack lacked.\n448\t- ~~re-run all Java cross_doc_link runs on their FINAL `best_model.pt`.~~ **DONE 2026-07-23**:\n449\t  all 4 traversals (bfs/dfs/rw/random) evaluated on final ckpts (step 14000–14750, val_loss≈1.05).\n450\t  Final Δnll +0.0646/+0.0738/+0.0788/+0.0314 — all positive, graph traversals cluster, random\n451\t  weakest. Supersedes the provisional (undertrained-ckpt) numbers. See RESULTS.md + RESULTS_code_crossdoc.md;\n452\t  per-run `runs/<id>/eval_java_repobench_final.json`.\n453\t- **Go** has no RepoBench variant → survey the internet for a RepoBench-analogous Go\n454\t  cross-file dataset that can be hacked to expose import edges as cross-doc aux DocSpans;\n455\t  else fall back to the self-built test_community benchmark (filed below).\n456\t- Fold in TypeScript too — RepoBench has no TS variant either; same survey/self-built path.\n457\t\n458\t### Self-built cross-doc code benchmark from test_community splits (future — filed 2026-07-23)\n459\tExternal RepoBench only exists for Python + Java. For the other languages (Go, TS, Rust,\n460\tKotlin, Dart, Zig, JS) with no upstream cross-file benchmark, build one from our OWN\n461\theld-out `test_community` splits (the import-graph neighborhoods we already carve). Two\n462\ttoken-scope variants considered (human is most interested in these two):\n463\t  1. **Import-dependent tokens only** — score ONLY tokens that actually use an\n464\t     imported/cross-file symbol (identifiers resolved to the linked doc, or the line\n465\t     following an import reference), not all body tokens. This is why the current\n466\t     `community_pack_perplexity` is near-noise: it dilutes the cross-doc signal across\n467\t     every body token of dense/predictable import neighborhoods. Restricting to the\n468\t     import-consuming spans should recover a discriminating Δnll (this is the same token\n469\t     scope RepoBench's \"next_line\" achieves, just carved from our own graph instead of an\n470\t     external dataset).\n471\t  3. **Whole-body on sparser communities** — keep whole-doc scoring but curate to\n472\t     sparser / high-out-degree communities where each import carries more predictive\n473\t     weight. Simplest change to the existing metric; may still be diluted.\n474\tBoth reuse `score_completion_with_context_docs` (already language-agnostic — takes any\n475\t`link_detector`) + the per-language `test_community` split; no external dataset, no\n476\tannotator/injection. Sequencing: do the external Java RepoBench port FIRST (below), then\n477\tsurvey the internet for RepoBench-analogous cross-file datasets for the other languages\n478\tthat can be similarly hacked to expose import edges as cross-doc aux DocSpans; fall back\n479\tto this self-built path only where none exist.\n480\t\n481\t### Dataset-side dedup blacklist + rebuild/retrain (deferred — filed 2026-07-24)\n482\tThe external-benchmark ports (Go←CoLT-132K, TS←CrossCodeEval, Kotlin←ASE-2025;\n483\tsee `docs/crossdoc_benchmark_port_harness_DESIGN.md`) enforce hard dedup on the\n484\tBENCHMARK side: repo-name intersection with our training corpora (a repo lives in\n485\tthe dataset or the benchmark, never both) + file-hash exclusion of cross-repo\n486\tcopy-pastes. If exclusion ever guts a benchmark below Tier-2 power (n_cross_doc≥200),\n487\tthe fix is the inverse direction: add a repo blacklist stage to the dataset pipeline\n488\t(`data/stack_sharded_download.py` / graph builders), rebuild the affected language\n489\tdataset, regenerate schedules (see schedule-staleness memory), and RETRAIN its sweep\n490\tckpts. Expensive — deferred until benchmark-side exclusion proves insufficient. Also\n491\trerun the repo intersection against Stack-v2 repo lists before any v2 retrain\n492\t(CrossCodeEval's 2023 repos fall inside v2's crawl window).\n493\t\n494\t### LLM conceptual-dependency audit of ALL cross-doc benchmarks (deferred — filed 2026-07-25)\n495\tThe port harness deliberately has NO syntactic \"target-uses-aux-symbol\" gate (rejected:\n496\tan example's dependency on its cross-file snippet can be conceptual — patterns,\n497\tinvariants, config values — with no in-line identifier match, so a tree-sitter check\n498\tis fragile in both directions). Instead, once ports exist, run a smarter LLM over the\n499\tENTIRETY of every cross-doc benchmark — the new ports AND the existing python/java\n500\tRepoBench ones — asking per example: \"does predicting this completion genuinely benefit\n501\tfrom the granted cross-file snippet(s), and how (symbol use / conceptual / not at all)