 $24$ layers, $8$ heads\n(head dimension $128$), $32{,}768$-token context, GPT-2 vocabulary, roughly $350$M\nparameters with the optional value-embedding banks disabled (\\Cref{app:recipe}). A\ntraining step is $8$ packs of $32$k tokens ($262{,}144$ tokens per optimizer step,\none pack per GPU, no gradient accumulation) on A100-80GB nodes under SLURM. The\nsingle-source runs train for about $15$k steps ($\\approx3.9$B tokens); on the\nWikipedia graph this is four passes over the training split, each pass a distinct\ntraversal (a different seed re-packs every document with a different subset and\norder of its neighbours), so repeated epochs re-sample the co-occurrence structure\nrather than replaying it.\n\n\\paragraph{Recipe.}\nThe optimizer and schedule are inherited (\\Cref{app:recipe}) but two hyperparameters\nwere retuned for this data regime because the inherited values, set for a short\nFineWeb speedrun, were badly off: an eight-point Muon learning-rate sweep on the\n\\doccausal Wikipedia model found a clean U-curve with its minimum at\n$\\mu_\\text{lr}=0.003$ (the inherited $0.001$ was second-worst), and a weight-decay\nsweep found the inherited $1.2$ roughly an order of magnitude too high, with the\nminimum at $0.1$. AdamW's rate is coupled at $0.01\\times$ Muon's. Unless stated\notherwise every run uses $\\mu_\\text{lr}=0.003$, warmup $300$ steps, a linear cooldown\nover the final $40\\%$ to $0.1\\times$ peak, the embedding/head untie at two-thirds of\ntraining, and $\\texttt{max\\_grants}=256$. The weight decay of each arm is listed with\nits result (\\Cref{app:results}); within a paired contrast both arms share every\nhyperparameter, so the within-model $\\Delta$ is robust to the exact operating point.\n% Provenance for the sweeps: RESULTS.md \"LR RETUNE MILESTONE\" + \"Follow-on tuning\";\n% TUNING_LESSONS.md. The sweep U-curve values are training-log val losses and are not\n% yet ledger-grounded (see the log-source item in TODOS.md).\n\n\\paragraph{The cross-document contrast.}\nOur headline metric is a paired, same-token contrast. For a benchmark item with a\ncontext $c$, a completion $y$, and a document $B$ that $c$ links to, we score the\nteacher-forced negative log-likelihood of $y$ twice on the \\emph{same} model: once\nwith $B$ packed before $c$ and the \\crossdoc grant open from the link in $c$ into $B$\n(\\emph{cross-doc}), and once with the identical $c$ and $y$ scored in isolation under\n\\doccausal with no $B$ present (\\emph{flat}). We report\n$\\Delta\\mathrm{nll}=\\mathrm{nll}_\\text{flat}-\\mathrm{nll}_\\text{cross}$ over the\nsubset of items on which the link detector fires and resolves (the \\emph{fired}\nsubset, of size $n$), with bootstrap $95\\%$ confidence intervals. Because both arms\nscore the same tokens on the same weights, memorization of $y$ cancels: the contrast\nmeasures only what attending into $B$ through the link adds.\n\\begin{itemize}\n  \\item \\textbf{HotpotQA cross-doc} (Wikipedia). Bridge-type questions from HotpotQA\n        \\citep{yang2018hotpotqa}: the second supporting article $B$ is the target, the\n        first supporting article $A$---with its hyperlinks rewritten to Markdown\n        \\texttt{[text](Title)} form---is the context, followed by the question and\n        \\texttt{Answer:}; the completion is the answer. Items are kept only if some\n        sentence of $A$ links to $B$'s title, and two documented structural non-fires\n        (parenthesized and quoted titles) are left as non-fires rather than forced.\n  \\item \\textbf{RepoBench cross-doc} (Python, Java). From RepoBench\n        \\citep{liu2024repobench}: the item's cross-file snippets are the targets, the\n        import statement plus the cropped in-file code is the context, and the next\n        line is the completion. Each import path is matched precisely to its snippet\n        (Java imports are resolved to source-root-relative fully-qualified names).\n  \\item \\textbf{Internal cross-file ports} (the other code languages). For languages\n        with no external cross-file benchmark we build the same item shape from our own\n        held-out community splits and score at the \\emph{use line}---the first statement\n        that references a symbol declared in the granted file, located with a\n        tree-sitter declaration query---which removes import-syntax artefacts and\n        concentrates the signal where the dependency is used. These carry a\n        \\emph{placebo} arm: the same identifiers (so grants still fire) with the target\n        content swapped for another item's, testing whether the gain comes from the\n        \\emph{right} document or from any extra in-language tokens. The headline\n        HotpotQA and RepoBench arms do not yet carry this placebo\n        (\\Cref{sec:analysis}).\n  \\item \\textbf{Community-pack perplexity.} Packs built by BFS over a held-out\n        \\texttt{val\\_community} split (intact linked subgraphs, \\Cref{app:datasets}) are\n        scored under \\crossdoc versus \\doccausal on the same model, grants taken\n        directly from graph edges; $\\Delta\\mathrm{nll}$ is over documents with at least\n        one incoming grant. This is the metric of the density experiments below.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\paragraph{Single-document controls.}\nIsolated-document held-out perplexity and six likelihood-scored multiple-choice\nbenchmarks---HellaSwag \\citep{zellers2019hellaswag}, ARC-easy \\citep{clark2018arc},\nPIQA \\citep{bisk2020piqa}, WinoGrande, OpenBookQA, and BoolQ---scored under\n\\doccausal for every model. No link can fire on an isolated document, so these\nmeasure whether a mask condition changes the base language model at all.\n\n\\paragraph{Ablation designs.}\n\\emph{Four-mask}: \\doccausal, \\crossdoc, \\concatlink, \\concat on the Wikipedia graph\nwith identical data order and recipe. \\emph{Traversal}: $\\{$bfs, dfs, random walk,\nrandom$\\}\\times\\{\\doccausal,\\crossdoc\\}$ on Wikipedia (HotpotQA cross-doc) a