& \\val{crossdoc.wiki_hotpotqa.dfs.nll_flat} & \\val{crossdoc.wiki_hotpotqa.dfs.delta_nll} & \\val{crossdoc.wiki_hotpotqa.dfs.n} \\\\\n    Wiki HotpotQA & random walk & \\val{crossdoc.wiki_hotpotqa.rw.nll_crossdoc} & \\val{crossdoc.wiki_hotpotqa.rw.nll_flat} & \\val{crossdoc.wiki_hotpotqa.rw.delta_nll} & \\val{crossdoc.wiki_hotpotqa.rw.n} \\\\\n    Wiki HotpotQA & random      & \\val{crossdoc.wiki_hotpotqa.random.nll_crossdoc} & \\val{crossdoc.wiki_hotpotqa.random.nll_flat} & \\val{crossdoc.wiki_hotpotqa.random.delta_nll} & \\val{crossdoc.wiki_hotpotqa.random.n} \\\\\n    \\midrule\n    Java RepoBench & bfs         & \\val{crossdoc.repobench_java.bfs.nll_crossdoc} & \\val{crossdoc.repobench_java.bfs.nll_flat} & \\val{crossdoc.repobench_java.bfs.delta_nll} & \\val{crossdoc.repobench_java.bfs.n} \\\\\n    Java RepoBench & dfs         & \\val{crossdoc.repobench_java.dfs.nll_crossdoc} & \\val{crossdoc.repobench_java.dfs.nll_flat} & \\val{crossdoc.repobench_java.dfs.delta_nll} & \\val{crossdoc.repobench_java.dfs.n} \\\\\n    Java RepoBench & random walk & \\val{crossdoc.repobench_java.rw.nll_crossdoc} & \\val{crossdoc.repobench_java.rw.nll_flat} & \\val{crossdoc.repobench_java.rw.delta_nll} & \\val{crossdoc.repobench_java.rw.n} \\\\\n    Java RepoBench & random      & \\val{crossdoc.repobench_java.random.nll_crossdoc} & \\val{crossdoc.repobench_java.random.nll_flat} & \\val{crossdoc.repobench_java.random.delta_nll} & \\val{crossdoc.repobench_java.random.n} \\\\\n    \\midrule\n    Python RepoBench & bfs      & \\val{crossdoc.repobench_python.nll_crossdoc} & \\val{crossdoc.repobench_python.nll_flat} & \\val{crossdoc.repobench_python.delta_nll} & \\val{crossdoc.repobench_python.n} \\\\\n    \\bottomrule\n  \\end{tabular}\n  \\caption{Paired cross-doc vs.\\ flat mean NLL on the fired subset, per \\crossdoc\n  training arm. Wikipedia arms: \\texttt{wiki\\_merged}, $\\mu_\\text{lr}=0.003$,\n  $\\mu_\\text{wd}=0.3$, VE off; the bfs arm was scored on the full fired set\n  ($n=738$) and the other three on the $\\texttt{max\\_grants}=256$ re-evaluation subset\n  ($n=377$), so absolute NLLs are comparable within but not across those two groups.\n  Java arms: final checkpoints of the Java sweep; Python: \\texttt{max\\_grants}=256\n  re-evaluation of the \\texttt{thestack} \\crossdoc arm.}\n  \\label{tab:traversal}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Single-document benchmarks are unaffected}\n\\label{sec:results:singledoc}\nCross-document attention has nothing to attend to on an isolated document, and the\ncontrols confirm it does not change the base model (\\Cref{tab:singledoc-tuned}): at the\ntuned recipe the three recipe-matched masks are indistinguishable on isolated-document\nheld-out perplexity and on the two benchmarks these models clear chance on, HellaSwag\nand ARC-easy, with overlapping $95\\%$ intervals. The full four-mask panel (HellaSwag,\nARC-easy, PIQA, WinoGrande, OpenBookQA, BoolQ) on an earlier, undertrained matched set\ntells the same story at chance-level accuracy (\\Cref{tab:singledoc-4mask},\n\\Cref{app:results}). The claim is that the mask does not \\emph{degrade}\nsingle-document capability, not a strong-capability equivalence: these are small models\ntrained for a few billion tokens.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n  \\centering\n  \\small\n  \\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrr@{}}\n    \\toprule\n    & \\doccausal & \\crossdoc & \\concatlink \\\\\n    \\midrule\n    Held-out ppl $\\downarrow$ & \\val{singledoc_tuned.held_out_ppl.doc_causal} & \\val{singledoc_tuned.held_out_ppl.cross_doc_link} & \\val{singledoc_tuned.held_out_ppl.doc_concat_link} \\\\\n    HellaSwag & \\val{singledoc_tuned.hellaswag.doc_causal} \\val{singledoc_tuned.hellaswag.doc_causal.ci} & \\val{singledoc_tuned.hellaswag.cross_doc_link} \\val{singledoc_tuned.hellaswag.cross_doc_link.ci} & \\val{singledoc_tuned.hellaswag.doc_concat_link} \\val{singledoc_tuned.hellaswag.doc_concat_link.ci} \\\\\n    ARC-easy  & \\val{singledoc_tuned.arc_easy.doc_causal} \\val{singledoc_tuned.arc_easy.doc_causal.ci} & \\val{singledoc_tuned.arc_easy.cross_doc_link} \\val{singledoc_tuned.arc_easy.cross_doc_link.ci} & \\val{singledoc_tuned.arc_easy.doc_concat_link} \\val{singledoc_tuned.arc_easy.doc_concat_link.ci} \\\\\n    \\bottomrule\n  \\end{tabular}\n  \\caption{Single-document controls, recipe-matched masks on \\texttt{wiki\\_merged} at\n  $\\mu_\\text{lr}=0.003$, $\\mu_\\text{wd}=0.3$, VE off, bfs; brackets are $95\\%$\n  intervals. No \\concat arm was evaluated at this recipe.}\n  \\label{tab:singledoc-tuned}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Compute controls}\n\\label{sec:results:controls}\nGranting the baselines more within-context attention does not close the gap\n(\\Cref{tab:compute-controls}). On code RepoBench perplexity, \\crossdoc reaches\n\\val{compute.repobench_ppl.cross_doc_link}, whereas \\concatlink and \\concat --- which\nsee the same concatenated multi-document context and strictly more attention --- sit at\n\\val{compute.repobench_ppl.doc_concat_link} and \\val{compute.repobench_ppl.doc_concatenated}\nrespectively, and the plain \\doccausal baseline at \\val{compute.repobench_ppl.doc_causal}.\nThe residual that isolates the contribution is \\crossdoc against \\concatlink at\nidentical connectivity, and it favours the link-gated mask; \\concat, the most\npermissive condition, is the \\emph{worst} of the four, consistent with the packing\nliterature's finding that indiscriminate cross-document attention hurts. The matching\ncontrast on the Wikipedia HotpotQA benchmark --- \\concatlink scored cross-doc against\n\\crossdoc --- is \\fillin{$\\Delta$nll, wiki concat-link arm}.\n% The wiki_docconcatlink_best run (run_20260717_234605_156456) has single-doc evals but\n% NO hotpotqa_cross_doc eval in any record; needs a cluster-side eval (TODOS.md).\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n  \\centering\n  \\small\n  \\begin{tabular}{@{}lr@{}}\n    \\toprule\n    Mask & RepoBench perplexity $\\downarrow$ \\\\\n    \\midrule\n    \\doccausal  & \\val{compute.repobench_ppl.doc_causal} \\\\\n    \\crossdoc   & \\val{compute.repobench_ppl.cross_doc_link} \\\\\n    \\concatlink & \\val{compute.repobench_ppl.doc_concat_link} \\\\\n    \\concat     & \\val{compute.repobench_ppl.doc_concatenated} \\\\\n    \\bottomrule\n  \\end{tabular}\n  \\caption{Compute controls on The Stack (Python), VE off, one run per mask. Attention\n  permitted increases down the table from \\doccausal to \\concat; the link-gated\n  \\crossdoc is best and the most permissive control is worst.}\n  \\label{tab:compute-controls}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Traversal ablation}\n\\label{sec:results:traversal}\nGraph-structured packing matters for the base language model independent of the\ncross-document objective: final validation loss on Wikipedia is\n\\val{traversal.wiki_val_loss.graph} under graph (bfs/dfs) packing versus\n\\val{traversal.wiki_val_loss.random} under random packing. The incremental\ncross-document benefit is itself traversal-robust --- every Wikipedia and Java arm of\n\\Cref{tab:traversal} is positive, with random packing the weakest --- so packing\nquality and cross-document attention are two decoupled axes: traversal strongly moves\nbase-model loss and only weakly moves the within-model cross-document gain.\n% The two validation-loss figures are read from training logs, not the eval harness, and\n% are tracked as grounding debt until a log-value source lands; see provenance/README.md.\n\n\\subsection{The cross-document benefit scales with realized link density}\n\\label{sec:results:density}\nWith corpus, packing, and model held fixed and only the fraction $\\kappa$ of resolved\ngrants varied, the community-pack $\\Delta\\mathrm{nll}$ on every code graph rises\nmonotonically with $\\kappa$ at evaluation time, near-linearly, and is pinned to zero at\n$\\kappa=0$ (the \\doccausal identity; the instrument was verified to reproduce\n$\\Delta=0$ exactly on real checkpoints). Which edges are kept does not matter, only how\nmany: subsampling by edge and by node give the same lines. Pooling the nine code graphs\nand regressing $\\Delta\\mathrm{nll}$ on the number of grants the mask actually realizes\nper pack (out-degree $\\times$ co-packing rate $\\times\\kappa$) yields a single\ncross-dataset line with correlation \\fillin{$r$, eval-time, code} over\n\\fillin{$n$} points and slope \\fillin{nats per realized grant}, extrapolating above\ntoday's densest corpus. Raw graph out-degree is the wrong axis, with correlation\n\\fillin{$r$, out-degree}: it counts edges whose targets are never co-packed and hence\nnever attended.\n\nRetraining at each $\\kappa$ and re-evaluating at full density gives the train-time\nline, which is monotone but strongly \\emph{concave}: most of the benefit is already\npresent when the model trained on a quarter of the edges, and the train-time curve\nlies above the eval-time curve at low $\\kappa$ (training on a sparse graph beats\nevaluating a dense-trained model at that sparsity). The two-dimensional\ntrain-$\\kappa\\times$eval-$\\kappa$ grid makes the asymmetry explicit: moving along the\neval-density axis swings $\\Delta$ by \\fillin{eval-axis swing} while moving along the\ntrain-density axis moves it by only \\fillin{train-axis swing}. The limiting case is the\n$\\kappa_\\text{train}=0$ row --- a \\doccausal-trained model evaluated under the\n\\crossdoc mask --- which on TypeScript recovers $\\Delta\\mathrm{nll}=$\n\\fillin{dc-trained TS} against \\fillin{cdl-trained TS} for the model trained with\ngrants. On code, then, the cross-document benefit is overwhelmingly an\n\\emph{inference-time} effect: what the mask exposes at evaluation matters far more than\nwhether the model saw grants during training. The Wikipedia graph is excluded from\nthis analysis pending an audit of the community-pack instrument on that corpus\n(\\Cref{sec:analysis}).\n% NOT YET GROUNDED. Values from RESULTS_graph_sparsity.md (solo models only; unaffected\n% by the merged retraction). Artifacts: /fss-data/evin_t/tagseq2tagseq_artifacts/\n% sparsity_scaling/{phase1_eval,phase2_traintime,grid2d,effective_density.json,\n% regression.json}. To fill: eval-time code-only r=+0.95 (n=36), slope +0.00084/grant,\n% intercept +0.0005; train-time r=+0.84 (n=20), slope +0.00055, intercept +0.016;\n% out-degree r=-0.27; per-dataset eval Δ@κ=1: js +0.048 (67.1 grants/pack), ts +0.040\n% (48.8), rust +0.025 (21.1), dart +0.025 (26.8), python +0.020 (18.7), kotlin +0.011\n% (14.2), go +0.003 (5.4), java ~0 (3.1), zig -0.007 (5.1); 2D grid eval-axis swing\n% ~0.04-0.05 vs train-axis ~0.005; keep0-row TS dc-trained +0.041 vs cdl-trained +0.040\n% (preliminary; full 6-dataset row was SLURM 81104). Ground via a provenance `artifact`\n% source kind (TODOS.md); add a per-dataset Δ@κ table + the regression figure here once\n% grounded. Figures: sparsity_scaling/fig_sparsity.png, fig_traintime.png, fig_grid2d.png,\n% regression.png -- copy into paper/figures/.\n\n\\subsection{Diversity scaling (merged multi-source model)}\n\\label{sec:results:diversity}\nAn 11-source merged model's cross-document $\\Delta$ is \\fillin{ratio $\\times$}\n\\fillin{larger/smaller?} than single-domain specialists (flat scaling\n\\fillin{params} $\\to$ \\fillin{params}), suggesting joint training over many link types\n\\fillin{strengthens/weakens?} the cross-document machinery itself.\n% DO NOT ground yet: numbers are recomputable from results/merged_v2_{3p9b,8b}_ports_useline/*.json\n% (merge delta_real) + eval/benchmark_harness/reports/matrix/*.json (specialist baselines),\n% BUT the entire merged_all_v2 family was retracted 2026-08-14 (sequential-not-interleaved\n% dataloader bug; see RESULTS_graph_sparsity.md) and this claim is built on it. Kotlin/TS\n% baselines are external ports (indicative, not exact). Re-run on a fixed merge before grounding.\n% Add the per-port merge-vs-specialist table here once re-run.\n\n\\subsection{Density-aware scheduling efficiency}\n\\label{sec:results:efficiency}\nPrecomputing packs and scheduling by density buys wall-clock time twice: link\ndetection leaves the training step, and no rank stalls at the all-reduce waiting for a\ndenser pack. Measured on a two-node, two-GPUs-per-node run on The Stack at $32$k\ncontext (\\Cref{tab:efficiency}), mean step time drops from\n\\val{efficiency.step_time.live_mean}\\,s to \\val{efficiency.step_time.precomputed_mean}\\,s\n(a \\val{efficiency.speedup_mean}$\\times$ speedup) and worst-case step time from\n\\val{efficiency.step_time.live_max}\\,s to \\val{efficiency.step_time.precomputed_max}\\,s\n(\\val{efficiency.speedup_max}$\\times$). The live path's step-time distribution is broad\nand structureless (coefficient of variation \\val{efficiency.cov.live}\\%), while under\ndensity bucketing the within-bucket variation collapses to a mean of\n\\val{efficiency.cov.precomputed_within_bucket}\\% --- the remaining spread is\n\\emph{between} buckets, deterministic and density-driven (\\Cref{fig:density-timing}).\nThe rank-stall component is most visible across nodes and near zero on a single\nNVLink node.\n% Grounding status: these figures are ledger LITERALS (efficiency.*) copied from\n% README.md \"Measured impact\"; the step_timing_rank*.csv files are not located in-repo\n% and carry no run_id. Tracked as grounding debt; replace with a kind:artifact source\n% once the originating run (or a re-timing at the same layout) lands. See TODOS.md.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n  \\centering\n  \\small\n  \\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrr@{}}\n    \\toprule\n    & Live & Precomputed, density-bucketed & Speedup \\\\\n    \\midrule\n    Mean step time & \\val{efficiency.step_time.live_mean}\\,s & \\val{efficiency.step_time.precomputed_mean}\\,s & \\val{efficiency.speedup_mean}$\\times$ \\\\\n    Max step time  & \\val{efficiency.step_time.live_max}\\,s  & \\val{efficiency.step_time.precomputed_max}\\,s  & \\val{efficiency.speedup_max}$\\times$ \\\\\n    Step-time CoV  & \\val{efficiency.cov.live}\\%             & \\val{efficiency.cov.precomputed_within_bucket}\\% within-bucket & \\\\\n    \\bottomrule\n  \\end{tabular}\n  \\caption{Wall-clock effect of density-aware scheduling ($2$ nodes $\\times$ $2$ GPUs,\n  The Stack, $32$k context). ``Live'' traverses the graph, detects links, and builds\n  the mask online with random pack-to-rank assignment; ``precomputed'' uses offline\n  packs and draws every rank's pack from the same density bucket at each step.}\n  \\label{tab:efficiency}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n  \\centering\n  \\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{density_aware_timing.png}\n  \\caption{Per-step wall-clock time over a training run. Top: live training with online\n  link detection and random pack density --- step times vary widely with no structure.\n  Bottom: precomputed, density-bucketed training, steps coloured by density bucket ---\n  sparse buckets are fast, dense buckets slow, and the pattern is deterministic because\n  every rank draws from the same bucket at every step.}\n  \\label{fig:density-timing}\n\\end{figure}\n","file_path":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/tagseq2tagseq/paper/sections/06_results.tex"},"name":"Write","type":"tool_use"}],"role":"assistant"},"context":{"branch":"main","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/tagseq2tagseq","dirty_fingerprint":"eb66eb2dce29bf19de8230295a45870e6fee8471808fbd0ce44a344536bce2b2","head":"5c15f9d0ec9630286ce8d7d66c05e849ae72097f","repo":"git@github.com:evintunador/tagseq2tagseq.git"},"conversation":{"id":"claude-code:5cbbe416-90e8-4a21-93e7-044acf862880","seq":506},"id":"ev1-67caf4a15c07557ae2e70ea4f4969b204d8a54701ff4d610c5635126261d1b4c","kind":"conversation_turn","occurred_at":"2026-08-26T16:10:49.802Z","producer":{"model":"claude-fable-5","session_id":"5cbbe416-90e8-4a21-93e7-044acf862880","source":"claude-code","source_version":"2.1.241","tool":"cledger","version":"0.25.0"},"raw":{"data":{"cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/tagseq2tagseq","effort":"high","entrypoint":"cli","gitBranch":"main","isSide