{"actor":{"id":"claude-fable-5","type":"agent"},"content":{"blocks":[{"caller":{"type":"direct"},"id":"toolu_01QJw1pkmUp6WdK7JrfbfU64","input":{"content":"\\section{Results}\n\\label{sec:results}\n\n% Numbers in this section are generated from provenance/ledger.yaml via\n% scripts/gen_values_tex.py and cited with \\val macros, so each traces to the run and\n% code state that produced it; see provenance/README.md. (Author note, not rendered.)\n\n\\subsection{Cross-document attention beats scoring without the target}\n\\label{sec:results:crossdoc}\nOn the Wikipedia HotpotQA cross-document benchmark, the \\crossdoc model (bfs) attains a\ncross-document negative log-likelihood of \\val{crossdoc.wiki_hotpotqa.nll_crossdoc}\nagainst \\val{crossdoc.wiki_hotpotqa.nll_flat} for the same answers scored flat, over\n$n=\\val{crossdoc.wiki_hotpotqa.n}$ fired query--target pairs: a reduction of\n$\\Delta\\mathrm{nll}=\\val{crossdoc.wiki_hotpotqa.delta_nll}$ nats per token. The effect\ncarries over to code, where dependencies are more local and the margins accordingly\nsmaller: on Java RepoBench cross-document completion, cross-document attention lowers\nNLL under every traversal --- from \\val{crossdoc.repobench_java.random.delta_nll}\n(random packing) to \\val{crossdoc.repobench_java.rw.delta_nll} (random walk), with\n\\val{crossdoc.repobench_java.bfs.delta_nll} (bfs) and\n\\val{crossdoc.repobench_java.dfs.delta_nll} (dfs) in between --- and Python RepoBench\ncross-doc shows $\\Delta\\mathrm{nll}=\\val{crossdoc.repobench_python.delta_nll}$\n($n=\\val{crossdoc.repobench_python.n}$). Full per-arm tables are in\n\\Cref{app:results}.\n\n\\subsection{Single-document benchmarks are unaffected}\n\\label{sec:results:singledoc}\nCross-document attention has nothing to attend to on an isolated document, and the\ncontrols confirm it does not change the base model. At the tuned recipe the three\nrecipe-matched masks are indistinguishable on isolated-document held-out perplexity\n(\\val{singledoc_tuned.held_out_ppl.doc_causal} \\doccausal,\n\\val{singledoc_tuned.held_out_ppl.cross_doc_link} \\crossdoc,\n\\val{singledoc_tuned.held_out_ppl.doc_concat_link} \\concatlink) and on the two\nbenchmarks these models clear chance on, HellaSwag\n(\\val{singledoc_tuned.hellaswag.doc_causal}, \\val{singledoc_tuned.hellaswag.cross_doc_link},\n\\val{singledoc_tuned.hellaswag.doc_concat_link}) and ARC-easy\n(\\val{singledoc_tuned.arc_easy.doc_causal}, \\val{singledoc_tuned.arc_easy.cross_doc_link},\n\\val{singledoc_tuned.arc_easy.doc_concat_link}), with overlapping $95\\%$ intervals\n(e.g.\\ \\val{singledoc_tuned.hellaswag.doc_causal.ci} vs\n\\val{singledoc_tuned.hellaswag.cross_doc_link.ci}). The full four-mask panel\n(HellaSwag, ARC-easy, PIQA, WinoGrande, OpenBookQA, BoolQ) on an earlier, undertrained\nmatched set tells the same story at chance-level accuracy\n(\\Cref{tab:singledoc-4mask}). The claim is that the mask does not \\emph{degrade}\nsingle-document capability, not a strong-capability equivalence: these are small models\ntrained for a few billion tokens.\n\n\\subsection{Compute controls}\n\\label{sec:results:controls}\nGranting the baselines more within-context attention does not close the gap. On code\nRepoBench perplexity, \\crossdoc reaches \\val{compute.repobench_ppl.cross_doc_link},\nwhereas \\concatlink and \\concat --- which see the same concatenated multi-document\ncontext and strictly more attention --- sit at \\val{compute.repobench_ppl.doc_concat_link}\nand \\val{compute.repobench_ppl.doc_concatenated} respectively, and the plain \\doccausal\nbaseline at \\val{compute.repobench_ppl.doc_causal}. The residual that isolates the\ncontribution is \\crossdoc against \\concatlink at identical connectivity, and it favours\nthe link-gated mask; \\concat, the most permissive condition, is the \\emph{worst} of the\nfour, consistent with the packing literature's finding that indiscriminate\ncross-document attention hurts. The matching contrast on the Wikipedia HotpotQA\nbenchmark --- \\concatlink scored cross-doc against \\crossdoc --- is\n\\fillin{$\\Delta$nll, wiki concat-link arm}.\n% The wiki_docconcatlink_best run (run_20260717_234605_156456) has single-doc evals but\n% NO hotpotqa_cross_doc eval in any record; needs a cluster-side eval (TODOS.md).\n\n\\subsection{Traversal ablation}\n\\label{sec:results:traversal}\nGraph-structured packing matters for the base language model independent of the\ncross-document objective: final validation loss on Wikipedia is\n\\val{traversal.wiki_val_loss.graph} under graph (bfs/dfs) packing versus\n\\val{traversal.wiki_val_loss.random} under random packing. The incremental\ncross-document benefit is itself traversal-robust: the Wikipedia HotpotQA\n$\\Delta\\mathrm{nll}$ is \\val{crossdoc.wiki_hotpotqa.dfs.delta_nll} (dfs),\n\\val{crossdoc.wiki_hotpotqa.rw.delta_nll} (random walk), and\n\\val{crossdoc.wiki_hotpotqa.random.delta_nll} (random) against the bfs headline above,\nand the Java cross-document $\\Delta\\mathrm{nll}$ stays positive across all four\ntraversals (\\Cref{tab:traversal}). Packing quality and cross-document attention are two\ndecoupled axes: traversal strongly moves base-model loss and only weakly moves the\nwithin-model cross-document gain.\n% The two validation-loss figures are read from training logs, not the eval harness, and\n% are tracked as grounding debt until a log-value source lands; see provenance/README.md.\n% The dfs/rw/random HotpotQA arms were scored at n=377 (max_grants=256 re-eval); the bfs\n% headline at n=738. Table in App. Z shows n per row.\n\n\\subsection{The cross-document benefit scales with realized link density}\n\\label{sec:results:density}\nWith corpus, packing, and model held fixed and only the fraction $\\kappa$ of resolved\ngrants varied, the community-pack $\\Delta\\mathrm{nll}$ on every code graph rises\nmonotonically with $\\kappa$ at evaluation time, near-linearly, and is pinned to zero at\n$\\kappa=0$ (the \\doccausal identity; the instrument was verified to reproduce\n$\\Delta=0$ exactly on real checkpoints). Which edges are kept does not matter, only how\nmany: subsampling by edge and by node give the same lines. Pooling the nine code graphs\nand regressing $\\Delta\\mathrm{nll}$ on the number of grants the mask actually realizes\nper pack (out-degree $\\times$ co-packing rate $\\times\\kappa$) yields a single\ncross-dataset line with correlation \\fillin{$r$, eval-time, code} over\n\\fillin{$n$} points and slope \\fillin{nats per realized grant}, extrapolating above\ntoday's densest corpus. Raw graph out-degree is the wrong axis (\\fillin{$r$, out-degree}):\nit counts edges whose targets are never co-packed and hence never attended.\n\nRetraining at each $\\kappa$ and re-evaluating at full density gives the train-time\nline, which is monotone but strongly \\emph{concave}: most of the benefit is already\npresent when the model trained on a quarter of the edges, and the train-time curve\nlies above the eval-time curve at low $\\kappa$ (training on a sparse graph beats\nevaluating a dense-trained model at that sparsity). The two-dimensional\ntrain-$\\kappa\\times$eval-$\\kappa$ grid makes the asymmetry explicit: moving along the\neval-density axis swings $\\Delta$ by \\fillin{eval-axis swing} while moving along the\ntrain-density axis moves it by only \\fillin{train-axis swing}. The limiting case is the\n$\\kappa_\\text{train}=0$ row --- a \\doccausal-trained model evaluated under the\n\\crossdoc mask --- which on TypeScript recovers $\\Delta\\mathrm{nll}=$\n\\fillin{dc-trained TS} against \\fillin{cdl-trained TS} for the model trained with\ngrants. On code, then, the cross-document benefit is overwhelmingly an\n\\emph{inference-time} effect: what the mask exposes at evaluation matters far more than\nwhether the model saw grants during training. The Wikipedia graph is excluded from\nthis analysis pending an audit of the community-pack instrument on that corpus\n(\\Cref{sec:analysis}).\n% NOT YET GROUNDED. Values from RESULTS_graph_sparsity.md (solo models only; unaffected\n% by the merged retraction). Artifacts: /fss-data/evin_t/tagseq2tagseq_artifacts/\n% sparsity_scaling/{phase1_eval,phase2_traintime,grid2d,effective_density.json,\n% regression.json}. To fill: eval-time code-only r=+0.95 (n=36), slope +0.00084/grant,\n% intercept +0.0005; train-time r=+0.84 (n=20), slope +0.00055, intercept +0.016;\n% out-degree r=-0.27; per-dataset eval Δ@κ=1: js +0.048 (67.1 grants/pack), ts +0.040\n% (48.8), rust +0.025 (21.1), dart +0.025 (26.8), python +0.020 (18.7), kotlin +0.011\n% (14.2), go +0.003 (5.4), java ~0 (3.1), zig -0.007 (5.1); 2D grid eval-axis swing\n% ~0.04-0.05 vs train-axis ~0.005; keep0-row TS dc-trained +0.041 vs cdl-trained +0.040\n% (preliminary; full 6-dataset row was SLURM 81104). Ground via a provenance `artifact`\n% source kind (TODOS.md). Figures: sparsity_scaling/fig_sparsity.png, fig_traintime.png,\n% fig_grid2d.png, regression.png -- copy into paper/figures/ once grounded.\n\n\\subsection{Diversity scaling (merged multi-source model)}\n\\label{sec:results:diversity}\nAn 11-source merged model's cross-document $\\Delta$ is \\fillin{ratio $\\times$}\n\\fillin{larger/smaller?} than single-domain specialists (flat scaling\n\\fillin{params} $\\to$ \\fillin{params}), suggesting joint training over many link types\n\\fillin{strengthens/weakens?} the cross-document machinery itself.\n% DO NOT ground yet: numbers are recomputable from results/merged_v2_{3p9b,8b}_ports_useline/*.json\n% (merge delta_real) + eval/benchmark_harness/reports/matrix/*.json (specialist baselines),\n% BUT the entire merged_all_v2 family was retracted 2026-08-14 (sequential-not-interleaved\n% dataloader bug; see RESULTS_graph_sparsity.md) and this claim is built on it. Kotlin/TS\n% baselines are external ports (indicative, not exact). Re-run on a fixed merge before grounding.\n\n\\subsection{Density-aware scheduling efficiency}\n\\label{sec:results:efficiency}\nDensity-aware batching yields a \\fillin{mean $\\times$} mean / \\fillin{max $\\times$} max\nstep-time \\fillin{speedup/slowdown?} over live training with online link detection and\nrandom density assignment, with within-bucket step-time coefficient of variation\n\\fillin{$\\downarrow$/$\\uparrow$?} (\\Cref{fig:density-timing}). Part of the mean gain is\nthe removal of online link detection from the step and part is the removal of the\nrank stall; the latter is most visible across nodes and near zero on a single NVLink\nnode.\n% Wall-clock timing (step_timing_rank*.csv), not the eval harness. Raw CSVs are not located\n% in-repo and carry no run_id; only README.md prose + docs/images/density_aware_timing*.png\n% survive. Indicative only until the originating run + its CSVs are identified.\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n  \\centering\n  \\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{density_aware_timing.png}\n  \\caption{Per-step wall-clock time over a training run. Top: live training with online\n  link detection and random pack density --- step times vary widely with no structure.\n  Bottom: precomputed, density-bucketed training, steps coloured by density bucket ---\n  sparse buckets are fast, dense buckets slow, and the pattern is deterministic because\n  every rank draws from the same bucket at every step.}\n  \\label{fig:density-timing}\n\\end{figure}\n","file_path":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/tagseq2tagseq/paper/sections/06_results.tex"},"name":"Write","type":"tool_use"}],"role":"assistant"},"context":{"branch":"main","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/tagseq2tagseq","dirty_fingerprint":"eb66eb2dce29bf19de8230295a45870e6fee8471808fbd0ce44a344536bce2b2","head":"5c15f9d0ec9630286ce8d7d66c05e849ae72097f","repo":"git@github.com:evintunador/tagseq2tagseq.git"},"conversation":{"id":"claude-code:5cbbe416-90e8-4a21-93e7-044acf862880","seq":308},"id":"ev1-defa865d396506142be1843b3d66964deeeab48f3ba6c471e29fbc7cf2298bb4","kind":"conversation_turn","occurred_at":"2026-08-