iki. But `RESULTS_graph_sparsity.md` §4 says solo-wiki cross-doc is *consistently negative* on community_pack (−0.02, tight CIs, audited as real, fit-dependent). Those are different metrics (benchmark cross-vs-flat-linked vs held-out mask-on/off), but a reviewer will put them side by side. You need to decide how the paper reconciles \"helps on bridge-reasoning QA, hurts on generic held-out wiki text\" — that's a scientific position, not an editing task.\n2. **\"Three edge modalities\" is not supported for arXiv.** There's no arXiv cross-doc benchmark result anywhere, arXiv's sparsity Δ is flat (+0.001), and arXiv `cross_doc_link` val loss (2.471) is *worse* than `doc_causal` (2.156). The intro bullet and abstract currently promise three modalities. Either scope to wiki+code, or an arXiv cross-doc benchmark has to exist.\n3. **Does the sparsity scaling law go in this paper?** It's the most complete result line in the repo (r=+0.95 cross-dataset density law for code, 2D grid, train-vs-eval density) and it's absent from the paper. Its key finding — a `doc_causal`-trained model gets nearly the full cross-doc benefit at inference (+0.041 vs +0.040 on TS), training saturates by ~25% of links — also *cuts against* the \"train on structure\" framing in the framing notes. Including it changes the thesis; excluding it leaves the strongest evidence out. Your call.\n4. **HotpotQA has no placebo/derangement control** (TODOS, filed 08-10). The cross arm sees strictly more tokens than flat; HotpotQA is partly single-hop solvable. The headline number is exposed until that's run (it's a pure eval, no retrain — cheap, but cluster-side).\n5. **Compute-controls paragraph is code-only** (thestack RepoBench ppl). The framing notes say `cdl − concat_link` is *the* contribution-isolating number. Is there a wiki concat control at the tuned recipe? RESULTS.md mentions all masks ≈ ppl 3.84 on single-doc but I don't see a wiki HotpotQA concat-arm number grounded.\n6. Authors, venue/format (sti